let __v_503 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"brake light\",\"brake warning\",\"handbrake\",\"parking brake\",\"brake fluid\"]},\"A red brake light usually means the handbrake is still on or the fluid is low, so check the lever first, then the fluid level under the bonnet. If the light stays on with the lever down, or the pedal feels soft, do not drive it: that is the brakes, and it needs looking at before you go anywhere.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"abs light\",\"abs warning\",\"anti-lock\",\"anti lock\"]},\"An ABS light means the anti-lock system has shut itself off, so the ordinary brakes still work and you can drive gently to a garage - it is what stops the wheels locking in a skid, so do not leave it for months. The usual causes are a wheel sensor or its ring, and the garage reads the fault code to say which.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"dpf light\",\"dpf warning\",\"particle filter\",\"diesel particle\",\"exhaust filter\"]},\"A DPF light means the diesel particle filter is part blocked; a steady light often clears after a 20-minute run at 50 mph or so in a lower gear, which gets it hot enough to burn the soot off. If it stays on, or the engine light joins it, drive to a garage instead of carrying on - a filter that cannot regenerate is an expensive part.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"airbag light\",\"air bag\",\"srs light\",\"airbag\"]},\"An airbag light means the system has a fault and the bags may not fire, so the car drives but the crash protection is not there; get the code read within a few weeks. The common causes are a connector under a seat or the clock spring in the steering wheel, and both are cheap compared with the alternative.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"battery light\",\"battery\",\"charging\",\"alternator\"]},\"A battery light on while you drive means the alternator is not charging, so you are running on what the battery holds - 20 or 30 miles at most with the lights off. Switch off anything electric, drive straight to a garage or stop somewhere safe and call for help.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tyre pressure light\",\"tpms\",\"tyre light\",\"tire pressure\",\"tire light\",\"flat tyre\",\"flat tire\",\"tyre pressure\",\"tyre\",\"tire\"]},\"A tyre pressure light means one tyre is below where it should be: check all four and the spare with a gauge and pump them to the figures on the sticker inside the driver's door. A light that comes back within a day is a slow puncture, not a sensor.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"power steering\",\"steering light\"]},\"A power steering light means the assistance has dropped out or is failing, so the wheel will feel heavy at parking speed; drive gently to a garage. If the car has a hydraulic system, check the fluid first - on an electric one, only a fault code will say which part has gone.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"traction light\",\"traction control\",\"esp light\",\"esc light\",\"skid light\",\"slippery\"]},\"A traction light that flashes and then goes out is the system working on a slippery road; one that stays on means it has switched itself off with a fault. The car still drives, but without that safety net, so book it in rather than waiting for a wet morning.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"glow plug\",\"glowplug\",\"preheat\"]},\"A glow plug light that stays on or flashes means the pre-heat system or the engine management has a fault, and on many diesels a flashing one means limp mode. Get the code read rather than revving through it, especially in cold weather when the plugs are doing the starting.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"adblue\",\"ad blue\",\"urea light\"]},\"An AdBlue light means the tank is getting low, so top it up at a pump or with a can - and do not let the countdown reach zero, because many cars then refuse to restart. Use the grade the handbook names, and wipe up any spills, since the fluid is corrosive to metal.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"service light\",\"spanner\",\"service indicator\",\"service due\"]},\"A spanner or service light is the car counting down to a service, not a fault, so it is safe to drive until the work is done. If the car has just been serviced, the garage forgot to reset it, and the handbook shows the button sequence.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"washer fluid\",\"washer warning\",\"screen wash\",\"washer light\"]},\"That symbol is the screen wash running low: top it up with screen wash and water, not washing-up liquid, which foams and clogs the jets. It is fine to drive, but do it before a salty motorway run rather than after.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"seatbelt\",\"seat belt\",\"belt light\"]},\"A seatbelt light that stays on means the car thinks a belt is unbuckled, so check the passenger seats and anything heavy left on them - a bag sets it off. If every belt is clipped and it is still lit, the buckle sensor is the part to look at.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"brake pad\",\"pad wear\",\"wear indicator\"]},\"A brake pad light means a sensor has worn through, so the pads are near the end of their life and want changing within a few hundred miles. Book it in now; waiting for the grinding noise means new discs as well as pads.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"key symbol\",\"immobiliser\",\"immobilizer\",\"key light\",\"key warning\"]},\"A key symbol light is the immobiliser not recognising the key - try the spare first. If the spare works, the first key's chip or battery is the problem, and a new key is cheaper from a locksmith than from the dealer.\"]}]}")?;
